From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: psci: run the init after deferred probing has settled

psci_idle_init() is a device_initcall, and device_initcall runs before
late_initcall. deferred_probe_initcall() is a late_initcall. On any
platform where the psci-cpuidle-domain driver defers on its first
attempt, cpuidle is therefore lost for the entire boot.

That is not hypothetical. On SM6375 (Motorola Moto G82 5G) the CPU
cluster power domain's parent is the MPM node -- power-domain-cpu-cluster0
has power-domains = <&mpm>, an ordinary #power-domain-cells = <0>
provider that has not probed yet at core_initcall time:

	[0.109751] CPUidle PSCI: failed to create CPU PM domains ret=-517
	[0.373495] CPUidle PSCI: CPU 0 failed to PSCI idle
	[0.373539] CPUidle PSCI: Failed to create psci-cpuidle device
	[0.424671] CPUidle PSCI: Initialized CPU PM domain topology using OSI mode

-517 is -EPROBE_DEFER and the deferral resolves correctly, 51 ms too
late. In between, psci_idle_init() runs, psci_dt_cpu_init_topology() ->
dt_idle_attach_cpu() -> dev_pm_domain_attach_by_name() returns
-EPROBE_DEFER, and because the device is created with
faux_device_create() -- which has no deferred-probe support -- nothing
retries. The result is silent: every config option is enabled, the
device tree is complete, and the CPUs simply never leave WFI. It failed
on every boot examined on that device.

Move the initcall to late_initcall_sync. deferred_probe_initcall()
triggers and flushes the deferred queue twice before returning, so at
7s the power domains are guaranteed to be present. This is an ordering
guarantee from the initcall levels rather than a timing one, which is
what the faux device cannot provide for itself. Registering cpuidle this
late costs only plain WFI for the rest of boot.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
@@ -489,4 +489,31 @@
 
 	return 0;
 }
-device_initcall(psci_idle_init);
+/*
+ * late_initcall_sync, not device_initcall: this has to run after
+ * deferred_probe_initcall(), which is a late_initcall.
+ *
+ * psci_cpuidle_probe() attaches every CPU to its PSCI power domain, and on
+ * platforms where the CPU cluster domain has a parent that probes late the
+ * psci-cpuidle-domain driver is deferred on its first attempt. On SM6375 the
+ * cluster domain's parent is the MPM node, and the timestamps are unambiguous:
+ *
+ *	[0.109751] CPUidle PSCI: failed to create CPU PM domains ret=-517
+ *	[0.373495] CPUidle PSCI: CPU 0 failed to PSCI idle
+ *	[0.373539] CPUidle PSCI: Failed to create psci-cpuidle device
+ *	[0.424671] CPUidle PSCI: Initialized CPU PM domain topology using OSI mode
+ *
+ * The deferral resolves correctly 51 ms later; the problem is that
+ * device_initcall runs first, dev_pm_domain_attach_by_name() hands back
+ * -EPROBE_DEFER, and a faux device has no deferred-probe support, so nothing
+ * ever retries and cpuidle is lost for the whole boot. Deterministically: it
+ * failed on every boot examined.
+ *
+ * Running at late_initcall_sync puts this strictly after
+ * deferred_probe_initcall(), which triggers and flushes the deferred queue
+ * twice before returning, so the domains are guaranteed to exist by the time
+ * the CPUs are attached. That is an ordering guarantee from the initcall
+ * levels rather than a timing one. Registering cpuidle this late only means
+ * the CPUs use plain WFI for the remainder of boot.
+ */
+late_initcall_sync(psci_idle_init);
